==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|accessory}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əkˈsɛs(ə)ɹi/|/ækˈsɛs(ə)ɹi/|a=RP,GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-accessory.ogg|a=US}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|accessarie}}, {{m|enm|axcessary}}, {{m|enm|excercary}}, from {{der|en|ML.|accessārius}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.'' and ''adj.''|noformat=1|id=6161198645}}</ref><ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=accessō̆rie|pos=n|id=MED268}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} Someone who [[accedes]] to some act, now especially a crime; one who [[contributes]] as an [[assistant]] or [[instigator]] to the commission of an offense.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|accessaryship|{{l|en|accessary before the fact}} {{sense|legal}} one who commands or counsels an offense, not being present at its commission.|{{l|en|accessary after the fact}} {{sense|legal}} one who, after an offense, assists or shelters the [[offender]], not being present at the commission of the offense.}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|person}}
* Estonian: {{t|et|kaasosaline}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|ML.|accessārius}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} [[accompany|Accompanying]] as a subordinate; additional; [[accessory]]; especially, uniting in, or [[contributing]] to, a crime, but not as chief [[actor]]. See [[accessory]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3 Q1|act=I|scene=ii|sig=B2|verso=1|page=20|passage=To both their deaths ſhalt thou be '''acceſſary'''.}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|accessarily|accessariness}}

===Usage notes===
* "This word, as used in law, is spelt ''accessory'' by Blackstone and many others; but in this sense is spelt ''accessary'' by Bouvier, Burrill, Burns, Whishaw, Dane, and the Penny Cyclopedia; while in other senses it is spelt ''accessory''. In recent [[textbook|text-books]] on criminal law the distinction is not [[preserved]], the spelling being either ''accessary'' or ''accessory''." - ''Webster, 1913''. Since that time this trend has accelerated.

